Excited atoms can emit light at particular wavelengths, and a material also absorbs different wavelengths differently.
Red filter glass transmits light near the red end more readily than some other bands. The source uses $\lambda=8\times10^{-5}$ cm as an illustrative red-end value;
this is near the infrared region, not a strict boundary of visible red light.

When white light passes through a cooler, dilute gas, dark lines appear on the continuous spectrum;
the source shows examples 5—8 in color plate V.
The absorption and emission lines of the same gas occur at corresponding wavelengths. **Wavelengths emitted by an excited gas can also be selectively absorbed from a continuous beam**, producing an absorption spectrum.

Absorption lines in solar and stellar spectra help identify elements in their outer gas layers.
The solar photosphere emits an approximately continuous spectrum. Cooler outer gas selectively absorbs some wavelengths and produces dark lines;
these lines are not a direct, complete measurement of the Sun’s deep interior.
